# The boot-protocol keyboard HID report descriptor, 63 bytes.
#
# Checked in as annotated hex rather than as a binary blob, on purpose:
# the structure of a HID descriptor is the thing worth reading, and a
# .bin in a repository is 63 bytes nobody will ever look at again.
#
# The recipe converts this to bytes AT BUILD TIME and installs the
# result, so the board needs no hex tool. The specification converts it
# on the board with "xxd -r -p", which assumes xxd is present; on a
# BusyBox image that is not a safe assumption, and a gadget that fails to
# build its report descriptor fails at the last step, after everything
# else looked right.
#
# The recipe also asserts the length is exactly 63. A descriptor that is
# short by a byte is not a syntax error: it is a tree the host parses
# differently, and the symptom is a keyboard that enumerates and types
# nothing.
#
# This is the descriptor from the HID 1.11 specification that every BIOS
# understands. Read it as two streams: Input items are what the device
# SENDS (8 bytes), Output items are what the host SENDS BACK (1 byte of
# LED state, readable from /dev/hidg0).

05 01        # Usage Page (Generic Desktop)
09 06        # Usage (Keyboard)
a1 01        # Collection (Application)

# ---- byte 0: the eight modifier keys, one bit each ----------------------
05 07        #   Usage Page (Key Codes)
19 e0        #   Usage Minimum (224) = Left Control
29 e7        #   Usage Maximum (231) = Right GUI
15 00        #   Logical Minimum (0)
25 01        #   Logical Maximum (1)
75 01        #   Report Size (1 bit)
95 08        #   Report Count (8)
81 02        #   Input (Data, Variable, Absolute)

# ---- byte 1: reserved, always zero --------------------------------------
95 01        #   Report Count (1)
75 08        #   Report Size (8 bits)
81 03        #   Input (Constant): the reserved byte

# ---- host to device: five LED bits plus three of padding ----------------
95 05        #   Report Count (5)
75 01        #   Report Size (1 bit)
05 08        #   Usage Page (LEDs)
19 01        #   Usage Minimum (1) = Num Lock
29 05        #   Usage Maximum (5) = Kana
91 02        #   Output (Data, Variable, Absolute): the LED report
95 01        #   Report Count (1)
75 03        #   Report Size (3 bits)
91 03        #   Output (Constant): padding to a whole byte

# ---- bytes 2 to 7: up to six simultaneous key usages --------------------
95 06        #   Report Count (6)
75 08        #   Report Size (8 bits)
15 00        #   Logical Minimum (0)
25 65        #   Logical Maximum (101)
05 07        #   Usage Page (Key Codes)
19 00        #   Usage Minimum (0)
29 65        #   Usage Maximum (101)
81 00        #   Input (Data, Array): the six key slots

c0           # End Collection
